"Empty search results\nNo results were found for the provided queries--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09710v1] Echoing: Identity Failures when LLM Agents Talk to Each Other (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09710v1)\nciteturn2333view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09710v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 3 Mar 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:Echoing: Identity Failures when LLM Agents Talk to Each Other\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Sarath Shekkizhar , cite7†Romain Cosentino , cite8†Adam Earle , cite9†Silvio Savarese L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Echoing: Identity Failures when LLM Agents Talk to Each Other, by Sarath Shekkizhar and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:As large language model (LLM) based agents interact autonomously with one another, a new class of failures emerges that cannot be predicted from single agent performance: behavioral drifts in agent-agent conversations (AxA). Unlike human-agent interactions, where humans ground and steer conversations, AxA lacks such stabilizing signals, making these failures unique.\nL17: We investigate one such failure, echoing, where agents abandon their assigned roles and instead mirror their conversational partners, undermining their intended objectives. Through experiments across $60$ AxA configurations, $3$ domains, and $2000+$ conversations, we demonstrate that echoing occurs across three major LLM providers, with echoing rates from $5\\%$ to $70\\%$ depending on the model and domain.\nL18: Moreover, we find that echoing is persistent even in advanced reasoning models with substantial rates ($32.8\\%$) that are not reduced by increased reasoning efforts. We analyze prompt impacts, conversation dynamics, showing that echoing arises as interaction grows longer ($7+$ turns in experiments) and is not merely an artifact of sub-optimal prompting. Finally, we introduce a protocol-level mitigation in which targeted use of structured responses reduces echoing to $9\\%$.\nL19: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.09710 [cs.AI]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.09710v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09710†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Sarath Shekkizhar [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 20:17:10 UTC (1,821 KB)\nL27: cite16†[v2] Thu, 15 Jan 2026 19:11:07 UTC (2,064 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v3] Tue, 3 Mar 2026 17:39:30 UTC (2,064 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Echoing: Identity Failures when LLM Agents Talk to Each Other, by Sarath Shekkizhar and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AI\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: load