Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Uncertainty-Guided Checkpoint Selection for Reinforcement Finetuning of Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Manh Nguyen , cite6†Dung Nguyen , cite7†Dai Do , cite8†Svetha Venkatesh , cite9†Hung Le L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Uncertainty-Guided Checkpoint Selection for Reinforcement Finetuning of Large Language Models, by Manh Nguyen and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Reinforcement learning (RL) finetuning is crucial to aligning large language models (LLMs), but the process is notoriously unstable and exhibits high variance across model checkpoints. In practice, selecting the best checkpoint is challenging: evaluating checkpoints on the validation set during training is computationally expensive and requires a good validation set, while relying on the final checkpoint provides no guarantee of good performance.\nL17: We introduce an uncertainty-guided approach for checkpoint selection (UGCS) that avoids these pitfalls. Our method identifies hard question-answer pairs using per-sample uncertainty and ranks checkpoints by how well they handle these challenging cases. By averaging the rewards of the top-uncertain samples over a short training window, our method produces a stable and discriminative signal without additional forward passes or significant computation overhead.\nL18: Experiments across three datasets and three LLMs demonstrate that it consistently identifies checkpoints with stronger generalization, outperforming traditional strategies such as relying on training or validation performance. These results highlight that models solving their hardest tasks with low uncertainty are the most reliable overall.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.09864 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.09864v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09864†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Manh Nguyen [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 13 Nov 2025 01:46:58 UTC (48 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Uncertainty-Guided Checkpoint Selection for Reinforcement Finetuning of Large Language Models, by Manh Nguyen and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.LG\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: \nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL73: \nL74: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL83: \nL84: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL85: \nL86: scite Smart Citations (cite30†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL87: \nL88: [Input] Cod